+# Agent Note: Chat asynchronous text link display
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-05-chat-async-text-links.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Resource recognition can require asynchronous metadata, while Chat's closing-file mention callback is synchronous and limited to a completed Turn's produced files. Resource plugins need to decorate existing text without replacing Chat, scanning the DOM, or rewriting the Session log.
+
+## Decision
+
+Chat consumes one optional `chatTextLinks` provider through stable callbacks created in `apply`. The provider owns recognition and routing. Primitives own only ordered UTF-16 ranges, inline buttons, and component-private asynchronous completion. A text or callback change, or unmount, invalidates pending results. Discovery rejection preserves ordinary projection; opening rejection reaches Chat's existing retry channel with the original target and operation kind.
+
+Settled Markdown resolves prose in `text` mode, parsed code spans in `inline-code` mode, and non-allowlisted authored link destinations in `target` mode. Both exact modes require one full-input range. Providers can restrict automatic filesystem discovery to real code spans without guessing from nearby backticks; escaped or unmatched backticks remain prose. Safe external URLs retain their hyperlinks. Link children remain non-interactive, and fence code and reasoning are outside discovery. Streaming keeps its incremental parser and cached blocks; new links appear after settlement. User text retains its reference-chip projection on uncovered segments and inside matched reference tokens.
+
+The [closing-file mention provider](../feature/2026-08-07-web-inline-file-mentions.md) retains its produced-file policy and supplies inline-code fallback. The [workspace-file waterfall](2026-09-05-chat-workspace-file-open-waterfall.md) owns existing file actions, including `.`; no native-only directory bypass exists. This note supplements both owners without replacing their policy or middleware rationale.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Parse resource paths in Chat or primitives.** Rejected because resource existence, metadata, and routing belong to the external plugin and must disappear with it.
+
+**Scan and mutate the rendered DOM.** Rejected because React and an external scanner would both own interactive elements, and streaming updates could invalidate injected handlers.
+
+**Resolve each growing reply in full.** Rejected because it discards the incremental renderer's bounded tail work. Settlement is the discovery point.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Source and model requests remain unchanged. Resolution results are local display state, not a cache shared across Sessions. Boot settles plugins before mounting views; a provider installed afterward decorates on the next resolution or remount. Removal is observed by each open or resolve callback, while already drawn controls report provider absence through the retry dialog. Resolving arbitrary text can issue many independent metadata requests; batching and metadata caching belong to the provider.
+
+Focused primitive and Chat tests cover asynchronous appearance, UTF-16 offsets, stale completion, rejection, safe hyperlinks, reference chips, retry routing, disposal, and the directory waterfall. Built browser composition and plugin metadata interpretation require the external plugin's integration evidence.
